# MPHam

MPHam provides spin–orbit-coupled DFT Hamiltonians in an atomic-orbital basis, used to train and test
[UniTB: Physical structure as an organizing principle for universal Hamiltonian learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36135).
All structures come from the [Materials Project](https://materialsproject.org/). The entry also holds the trained UniTB
model of the paper, its source code and a reproducible band-structure example (see "UniTB model weights, code and a
band-structure example").

| Subset               | Structures | Role                                                                                         |      Data | Archive parts |
| -------------------- | ---------: | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------: | ------------: |
| `MPHam-NextHam-test` |      3,000 | Test set: the full NextHAM test benchmark, with recomputed labels                            |  16.52 GB |             5 |
| `MPHam-12K`          |     11,953 | Training set: the NextHAM training list after deduplication                                  |  62.00 GB |            17 |
| `MPHam-29K`          |     29,242 | Training set: MPHam-12K plus 17,289 MP-20-derived structures                                 | 131.75 GB |            36 |
| `MPHam-hard`         |        500 | Benchmark: complex crystals newly added to the Materials Project, as DFT outputs (see below) | 195.88 GB |            52 |

Sizes are decimal GB (LMDB data for the first three subsets). MPHam-12K is contained in MPHam-29K; it is offered separately
for convenience (see "Using MPHam-12K from an MPHam-29K download" below). MPHam-hard is licensed differently from the rest
(see "License").

## Labels

The labels were computed with ABACUS using PBE, fully relativistic PseudoDojo ONCV pseudopotentials, spin–orbit coupling,
a 120 Ry cutoff and an SCF density threshold of 1e-6. For the NextHAM-list structures (MPHam-NextHam-test and MPHam-12K),
the k-point setting is as in Appendix H of NextHAM. The 17,289 MP-20-derived structures added in MPHam-29K use Γ-centred grids
with a k-point spacing of 0.08 bohr⁻¹.

Each record stores the real spin-up–spin-up block of H − H(0), H − P, H(0) and P on every atom and on every retained edge,
where P is the McWEDA-corrected non-self-consistent prior. The dimensionless
overlap S is stored in aligned sidecar shards. Energies are in eV; positions and cells are in Å.
Values are float32 with 729 shell-pair-packed slots per atom or edge; decode them with the supplied orbital map
(`basis.json`, `slot_map.json`), not as a row-major 27 × 27 matrix. Records contain only edges within the training cutoff and
cannot reconstruct the complete complex spinor Hamiltonian. `FORMAT.md` gives the full record format.

## Download and unpack

Files are named `<subset>.partNNN.tar`. Every part is a plain tar archive (the records inside are already zstd-compressed)
holding complete shards, main and overlap together, so the parts of a subset can be extracted in any order into one directory:

```bash
sha256sum -c MANIFEST.sha256 --ignore-missing      # check what you downloaded
for f in MPHam-29K.part*.tar; do tar -xf "$f"; done
```

This creates `MPHam-29K/train/data.train.sNNN.lmdb/data.mdb` and `MPHam-29K/overlap_sidecar/train/data.train.sNNN.lmdb/data.mdb`;
the other subsets follow the same pattern (`MPHam-NextHam-test/test/...`). `SHARD_MAP.json` gives the part, size and SHA-256 of
every shard, and `RECORDS.jsonl` lists every structure with its shard and shard-local index.

To read two records (requires `numpy`, `lmdb` and `zstandard`):

```bash
python read_example.py MPHam-NextHam-test/test/data.test.s000.lmdb \
  --overlap-shard MPHam-NextHam-test/overlap_sidecar/test/data.test.s000.lmdb --count 2
```

Only unpickle records after checking the files against `MANIFEST.sha256`.

## Using MPHam-12K from an MPHam-29K download

The 11,953 structures of MPHam-12K are 11,953 of the 29,242 training structures of MPHam-29K. With MPHam-29K downloaded,
`MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv` gives each of them its 29K shard and shard-local index, and `use_12k_from_29k.py` reads exactly those records:

```bash
python use_12k_from_29k.py --root MPHam-29K --list MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv --count 2
python use_12k_from_29k.py --root MPHam-29K --list MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv --all --overlap
```

The 12K structures are spread over 190 of the 229 training shards of MPHam-29K. Every numerical array of these records is
byte-identical in both subsets (checked for all 11,953 records); only the shard-local `idx` and the `provenance` field differ.

## Deduplication

The test set keeps all 3,000 NextHAM test entries. Two structures appear twice in it, once under a truncated Materials Project ID;
skipping `mp-16504` and `mp-63` gives the 2,998-entry deduplicated benchmark. The training sets exclude records that duplicate a
test structure or another training structure. `DEDUPLICATION.md`, `TRAINING_EXCLUSIONS.jsonl` and `NEXTHAM_TRUNCATED_IDS.jsonl` list
every case. In the `provenance` field of the training records, notes that pointed to internal working files now point to
`DEDUPLICATION.md`; all numerical arrays are byte-identical to the data used in the paper.

## MPHam-hard

`MPHam-hard` is a benchmark of 500 complex nonmagnetic crystals that the Materials Project added in its database release
v2026.04.13 (30–80 atoms and four or five elements per primitive cell; no identifier or reduced formula shared with
MPHam-29K or MPHam-NextHam-test). Unlike the three LMDB subsets it is distributed as DFT outputs: for every crystal an
ABACUS reference in the basis of the MPHam labels (H, S and the non-self-consistent prior H(0) as sparse matrices) and an
OpenMX reference in the basis of Uni-HamGNN (`openmx.scfout`), both with spin–orbit coupling on the same Γ-centred k mesh,
together with reference band energies, band-edge effective masses and the per-structure errors of every model reported on
MPHam-hard in the UniTB paper.

| Group               | Content                                                                                                                                                                  |      Size | Archive parts |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------: | ------------: |
| `MPHam-hard.meta`   | `MPHam-hard.csv`, structures, DFT settings with pseudopotentials and orbitals, reference eigenvalues, evaluation tables, readers, `README.md`, `FORMAT.md`, `SHA256SUMS` |   2.04 GB |             1 |
| `MPHam-hard.abacus` | ABACUS inputs and logs, H(R), S(R), H(0)(R)                                                                                                                              |  90.61 GB |            24 |
| `MPHam-hard.openmx` | OpenMX inputs, outputs and `openmx.scfout`                                                                                                                                | 103.23 GB |            27 |

```bash
for f in MPHam-hard.meta.part*.tar; do tar -xf "$f"; done      # add the .abacus / .openmx parts as needed
sha256sum -c MPHam-hard/SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing
```

The `meta` part alone is enough to run a model on the structures and compare its band energies with both references;
`MPHam-hard/README.md` explains which files inference, evaluation, recomputation and training need. A tool and a tutorial for
converting ABACUS outputs such as these into LMDB records like those of the other subsets will come with a later release of
the UniTB code.
Results reported on MPHam-hard should come from models that were not trained on it.

## UniTB model weights, code and a band-structure example

Three archives in this entry hold the trained UniTB model of the paper, its source code
and a reproducible band calculation.

| Archive                   | Size              | Content                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| ------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `UniTB.checkpoint.tar`    | 137,051,648 bytes | `UniTB_checkpoint/`: the onsite and hopping networks of UniTB trained on MPHam-29K with the H(0) prior (`unitb_mpham29k_onsite.pth`, `unitb_mpham29k_hopping.pth`, 8,025,099 parameters each), their training configurations and validation records, `MODEL_CARD.md`, `SHA256SUMS` |
| `UniTB.code.tar`          | 22,675,968 bytes  | `UniTB_code/`: the complete UniTB source code (repository DeePTB-Lab/UniTB, commit `d730e47`; Python package `dptb`), `INSTALL.md`, `LICENSE` (LGPL-3.0), `SHA256SUMS`                                                                                                             |
| `UniTB.example_bands.tar` | 6,705,152 bytes   | `UniTB_example_bands/`: `predict_bands.py`, the inputs for RbMgF3 (mp-8402), the DFT reference bands, the expected output and `README.md`                                                                                                                                          |

**The model.** UniTB predicts the self-consistent correction
Re[(H − H(0))↑↑] of the ABACUS Hamiltonian (real spin-up–spin-up block, eV) from the
zeroth-step Hamiltonian H(0) and the overlap S, in the 729-slot layout of the MPHam records
(`FORMAT.md` of the MPHam release). The complete spin–orbit Hamiltonian is H(0) plus this correction in both
spin-diagonal blocks. These weights give the paper's MPHam-29K results: on the NextHAM
benchmark (Table 1), a matrix error of 0.493 meV and a band error fw10 of 49.05 meV (mean
over 200 structures); on MPHam-hard (Table 2), 0.82 meV and 38.0 meV.

**Unpack and check.**

```bash
tar -xf UniTB.checkpoint.tar
tar -xf UniTB.code.tar
tar -xf UniTB.example_bands.tar
(cd UniTB_checkpoint && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS)
(cd UniTB_code && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS)
(cd UniTB_example_bands && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS)
```

**Install the code.** Python 3.10 and PyTorch 2.8.0 are needed. The commands below use
the CPU wheels; `UniTB_code/INSTALL.md` gives the CUDA variant and further notes.

```bash
# no venv module (Debian/Ubuntu without python3.10-venv)? use conda for the next two lines:
#   conda create -n unitb python=3.10 -y && conda activate unitb
python3.10 -m venv unitb-env
source unitb-env/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
pip install torch==2.8.0 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu
pip install torch-scatter==2.1.2 -f https://data.pyg.org/whl/torch-2.8.0+cpu.html
POETRY_DYNAMIC_VERSIONING_BYPASS=2.0.1+d730e47 pip install -e ./UniTB_code \
    torch_geometric==2.7.0 tqdm==4.67.1 e3nn==0.5.8 numpy==2.2.5 scipy==1.15.3 matplotlib==3.10.7
```

The archive has no git history, so `POETRY_DYNAMIC_VERSIONING_BYPASS` supplies the
package version.

**Run the example.** The script predicts the Hamiltonian of RbMgF3, one of the test
crystals in the paper's band figure. It then solves the bands with spin–orbit coupling on
the DFT k path and compares them with DFT. This takes about 25 s on a CPU:

```bash
cd UniTB_example_bands
python predict_bands.py
# RbMgF3 (mp-8402): fw10 UniTB = 13.5183 meV (paper 13.5186 meV, tolerance 0.050 meV: OK); H(0) alone = 982.6 meV
```

The figure is written to `output/bands_RbMgF3_mp-8402.png`. The last digit of fw10 can
differ with other PyTorch builds (float32 rounding): with the CUDA builds run on CPU it
reads 13.5182, on an NVIDIA L40S GPU 13.5183.
`python predict_bands.py --residual expected/residual.npz` runs only the band part, with
the saved prediction (it prints 13.5182).

**License and citation.** The weights and the example are released under CC BY 4.0, like
MPHam-12K, MPHam-29K and MPHam-NextHam-test. The code in `UniTB.code.tar` is licensed
under LGPL-3.0; its third-party parts keep their own licenses, which
`UniTB_code/INSTALL.md` lists. Please cite M. Liu, Z. Zhouyin, G. Wang, D. Zheng, L. Shen
and Q. Gu, *Physical structure as an organizing principle for universal Hamiltonian
learning*, arXiv:2610.36135.

## Files

| File                                                                                            | Content                                                           |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `MPHam-NextHam-test.partNNN.tar`, `MPHam-12K.partNNN.tar`, `MPHam-29K.partNNN.tar`              | LMDB shards with their overlap sidecars                           |
| `MPHam-hard.meta.part000.tar`, `MPHam-hard.abacus.partNNN.tar`, `MPHam-hard.openmx.partNNN.tar` | MPHam-hard (see above)                                            |
| `UniTB.checkpoint.tar`, `UniTB.code.tar`, `UniTB.example_bands.tar`                             | UniTB weights, source code and band-structure example (see above) |
| `README.md`, `FORMAT.md`                                                                        | This description and the full record format                       |
| `read_example.py`, `basis.json`, `slot_map.json`                                                | Reader and orbital maps                                           |
| `RECORDS.jsonl`, `SHARD_MAP.json`                                                               | Per-structure index and per-shard map                             |
| `MPHam-12K_in_29K.csv`, `use_12k_from_29k.py`                                                   | Reading MPHam-12K from MPHam-29K                                  |
| `DEDUPLICATION.md`, `TRAINING_EXCLUSIONS.jsonl`, `NEXTHAM_TRUNCATED_IDS.jsonl`                  | Deduplication record                                              |
| `MANIFEST.sha256`                                                                               | SHA-256 of every other file                                       |

## License

MPHam-NextHam-test, MPHam-12K and MPHam-29K are released under the
[Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).

MPHam-hard (its structures and the data computed on them) is released under the
[Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License (CC BY-NC 4.0)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/):
488 of its 500 structures come from the GNoME batch of the Materials Project, which marks them `BY-NC`
(`builder_meta.license` in `structures/<id>/mp_record.json`). The pseudopotential and orbital files in its `dft_settings/`
keep the terms of their sources, listed in `dft_settings/README.md`.

The UniTB weights and the band-structure example are released under CC BY 4.0. The UniTB source code in `UniTB.code.tar`
is licensed under LGPL-3.0; its third-party parts keep their own licenses, listed in `UniTB_code/INSTALL.md`.

Please cite UniTB and NextHAM when you use the data, follow the Materials Project citation guidance for the structures,
and cite A. Merchant et al., Nature 624, 80 (2023) for the GNoME structures in MPHam-hard.

